  "Question": "If \\(a,\\) \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are positive integers less than \\(10,\\) then \\((10a+b)(10a+c)\\) equals \\(100a(a+1)+bc\\) if:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(b+c=10\\)",
    "B": "\\(b=c\\)",
    "C": "\\(a+b=10\\)",
    "D": "\\(a=b\\)",
    "E": "\\(a+b+c=10\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Expanding the product reveals which terms must match:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(10a+b)(10a+c)\n&=100a^2+10ab+10ac+bc\\\\\n&=100a^2+10a(b+c)+bc.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe proposed expression is\n\\[\n100a(a+1)+bc=100a^2+100a+bc.\n\\]\nCanceling the matching terms shows that equality holds exactly when\n\\[\n10a(b+c)=100a.\n\\]\nBecause \\(a\\) is positive, \\(10a\\ne0\\), so division gives \\(b+c=10\\). Conversely, this condition makes the expanded expressions equal. The answer is \\(\\boxed{b+c=10}\\), choice A.",
